From edc8330d0e07f52e06437859b665fe98800c652d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 06:58:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 065/119] scene-referred S1: writer coverage - sky, emissive
 and the forward/transparent lever

Third item of Stage S1. The five m_fAdaptedSceneScaleLBuffer sites cover deferred lights, the sun,
sky ambient and the fog light list. Everything else that writes scene colour bypassed them, so with
item 2 live a stop of aperture moved the lit surfaces and left the sky, emissive meshes, particles,
water and fog volumes exactly where they were. This closes that.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s1-writer-coverage.md, approach A (one per-view exposure constant,
the UE View.PreExposure shape). Research: scene-notes/research/s1-adapted-scene-scale.md sections
5 and 7. Worked in the decision's order 1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 5; the cbuffer member (2) had to be
declared before emissive (3) could use it, so it lands in the same commit.

Coverage item 4, sky. CSkyStage::SetSkyParameters() / SetHDRSkyParameters() multiply every
radiance constant by the latched exposure: the skybox emittance, the procedural day sky's partial
Mie and Rayleigh in-scattering constants, the night sky base and delta colours, and the moon plus
both coronae (colour only - the corona .w is a falloff exponent, not a radiance). Every one of them
is linear in Stars.cfx, so a single multiply per constant is the whole change.
  Deviation from the decision note, deliberate: the note put this at the CPU fill
  (S3DEngineCommon::UpdateSkyInfo, Renderer.cpp:4007/4032). The fill runs on the MAIN thread; the
  sky stage runs on the render thread and can read the value latched for this exact render view,
  which is the entire point of item 1's latch. The stage also reaches both addends of the skybox
  emittance, the night sky, the moon and the day sky in one place, which the fill site does not.
  The 100x scale collision between the two addends is NOT touched (S2): the exposure multiplies
  their sum, so they move together and their ratio is unchanged.

Coverage item 3, emissive. Illum.cfx and MultiLayeredMaterials.cfx multiply the emittance by the
per-view exposure. Emissive is never in the G-buffer - emissive objects are re-queued forward and
drawn additively - so nothing else would ever have applied it. The duplicated (1000.0/10000.0)
literal in both files becomes one MAT_EMISSIVE_UNIT_SCALE in FXConstantDefs.cfi, matching the
symbolic form the C++ twin already uses (ShaderResources.h's kKiloScale / RENDERER_LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE).

Coverage item 2, the forward/transparent lever. PS_HDR_RANGE_ADAPT_MAX stops being the literal 1.0
and becomes a per-view constant: CV_SceneExposure, a new float4 at the end of CBPerViewGlobal in
both FXConstantDefs.cfi and Include_HLSL_CPP_Shared.h, filled in
CGraphicsPipeline::GeneratePerViewConstantBuffer() from the same latched value.
  The member stores exposure MINUS ONE and the macro adds it back:
  #define PS_HDR_RANGE_ADAPT_MAX (1.0 + CV_SceneExposure.x). That is load-bearing, not a flourish.
  Off the switch the exposure is 1.0, so the member is 0.0, so a shader whose per-view buffer is
  not bound reads 1.0 and multiplies by exactly 1.0 instead of blacking the surface out. Off the
  switch the picture is bit-identical (x * 1.0f is exact in IEEE); what changes is only that a
  multiply the compiler used to fold away now survives. The 16 multiply sites keep their text
  verbatim - effect layers, caustics, distance clouds x3, fog volumes x2, the particle imposter,
  all particles, the ocean, water fog x4, the water volume.
  The 5 sites that read scene colour BACK out of the HDR or reflection targets and divided the
  factor out again - Water.cfx:1053/1095, WaterReflectionsPass.cfi:137, WaterVolume.cfx:1147/1235 -
  now use PS_HDR_RANGE_ADAPT_MAX_SCENE_READ, which is 1.0. That round trip is the old console
  scheme's semantics and is exactly wrong for a pre-exposure: the value read is already exposed and
  must not be un-exposed. Off the switch it was 1.0 anyway, so those five fold away exactly as they
  do today.

Coverage item 5, decals: no site needed, and this settles the research note's open question 4
without a capture. Deferred decals write albedo/normal/gloss into the G-buffer and are then lit by
the deferred lights, so they take the exposure through the five sites. Forward (alpha-blended)
decals are drawn by Illum.cfx's %DECAL permutation - the same pixel shader whose emittance was just
changed - so they are covered by coverage item 3.

LEFT UNEXPOSED ON PURPOSE (D13, listed in scene-notes/log.md as S1 known breakage): sun shafts
(their colour is Normalize()d, which decouples them from the sun entirely - a pre-existing bug, S2),
volumetric clouds (own pipeline, own units), SVOGI (RGBM 4.0 ceiling, 8-bit pools) and the fog
density tuning (empirical, no unit scale anywhere). They run, they break visibly, and they are
fixed at the root in S2/S8.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): CryRenderD3D11, CinematicCamera.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A72MFkdSczncE7au9UVnmu
